Write ONE C# file: `F:\LocalVSProj\Visual Studio Apps\AntiQuickPad\product\Antipad\Calc\Calculator.cs` (create the Calc folder). It is the notepad-calculator engine for "Antipad", a Windows scratchpad app (net10.0, nullable enabled, implicit usings, no NuGet packages, no other files). Do not touch any other file in the repo. Do not build the whole project (other files are being written concurrently and may not compile); instead verify your file by creating a throwaway console project in `C:\Users\Neel\AppData\Local\Temp\claude\F--LocalVSProj-Visual-Studio-Apps-AntiQuickPad\966c1dc8-0742-4d57-af39-7b41c8396fd3\scratchpad\calctest` that links the file (`<Compile Include="...Calculator.cs" />`) and runs `Calculator.SelfTest`. Run it until all checks pass.

Like Soulver/Numi/AntiNote's math mode: a note whose first line is `math` evaluates every following line live and shows a coloured result at the line end.

## Required public API (exact names; namespace `Antipad.Calc`)
```csharp
namespace Antipad.Calc;
public enum TokenKind { Number, Identifier, Unit, Operator, Keyword, Comment, Function }
public readonly record struct CalcToken(int Start, int Length, TokenKind Kind);
public sealed record CalcResult(string Display, double Value, string? Unit);
public sealed class LineEval
{
    public CalcResult? Result { get; init; }          // null = no result to show (blank, comment, heading, error, incomplete)
    public IReadOnlyList<CalcToken> Tokens { get; init; } = [];  // for syntax colouring, offsets within the line
    public string? AssignedName { get; init; }        // set for `name = expr`
}
public static class Calculator
{
    // Evaluate a whole math note. lines[0] is the "math" keyword line (return null for it). Returns one entry per line.
    public static IReadOnlyList<LineEval?> EvaluateNote(IReadOnlyList<string> lines);
    // For ordinary notes: if the line ends with "=" (optionally trailing spaces) and the text before it is a valid expression
    // with at least one operator or function or unit conversion, return its result; otherwise null. No variables.
    public static CalcResult? EvaluateInline(string line);
    public static string Format(double value, string? unit);
    public static bool SelfTest(out string report);   // ~60+ assertions covering everything below; report lists failures
}
```
Must be fast (called on every keystroke for notes of ~200 lines): no regex compilation per call, no exceptions for normal invalid input on the hot path (a try/catch around a line is fine as a backstop). Pure, no I/O.

## Language
- Numbers: `12`, `1.5`, `.5`, `1,000,000` (comma thousands only when groups of 3), `1_000`, `1e3`, `0x1F`, suffixes `k` `M` `B`(billion only when directly attached to number and not a data unit context — simplest: support `k` and `M` only, attached, e.g. `5k`). Leading `$`, `€`, `£` currency marks the result unit as that symbol (no conversion), e.g. `$1200 / 3` → `$400`.
- Operators: `+ - * / ^ %(see below) ( )`, unary minus, `×` `÷`, `x` as multiply when between two numbers/expressions and not a variable named x, words `plus minus times`, `mod`. `^` right-assoc. Implicit multiplication `2(3+1)` and `2pi`.
- Percent: `20%` = 0.2 in multiplication context; `50 + 10%` = 55; `50 - 10%` = 45; `20% of 150` = 30; `15% off 80` = 68; `x as % of y` → percentage display e.g. `30 as % of 120` = `25%`. Display a bare percentage like `20%` as `20%`.
- Constants: `pi`, `π`, `e`, `tau`. Functions: sqrt, cbrt, abs, round(x[,digits]), floor, ceil, sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, log (base 10), ln, log2, exp, min(...), max(...), avg(...), pow(a,b). Trig in radians; also accept `sin(30 deg)` / `30°` converting degrees.
- Variables: `rent = 1200`, `rent: 1200` is NOT assignment (keep only `=`), names `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_ ]`? — keep names single-word identifiers (letters, digits, underscore; must start with letter/underscore). Later lines can use them; reassignment allowed; reactive (whole note re-evaluated each call). Unknown identifier → line has no result (but still tokenized).
- Keywords: `prev` / `ans` = previous line's result; `sum` / `total` = sum of results of the contiguous block of lines above (stop at a blank line or a heading/comment line); `avg` / `average` same block average. These keywords alone on a line, or usable in expressions (`total * 2`).
- Comments/headings: lines starting with `#` or `//` produce no result, tokens = one Comment token over the whole line. Text after `//` mid-line is a comment. Lines that are prose (e.g. `groceries for the week`) produce no result and must not throw.
- Labels: `Coffee: 4.50` → result 4.50 (text before a colon that isn't a valid expression is a label; tokenize label as Comment). Same with leading words: `lunch 12 + 3` → 15 is optional; colon form is required.
- Units with conversion `in` / `to` / `as`: length (mm cm m km in inch inches ft foot feet yd mi mile miles), mass (mg g kg lb lbs oz t), time (ms s sec second(s) min minute(s) h hr hour(s) day(s) week(s) year(s)), data (b bit B byte KB MB GB TB KiB MiB GiB — treat KB=1000, KiB=1024), volume (ml l L cup tbsp tsp floz gal), temperature (C F K, `°C` `°F`; affine conversion; `100 F in C` = 37.78 °C), speed (km/h kmh mph m/s), area (m2 m² ft2 sqft km2 acre ha). Arithmetic with compatible units converts to the left operand's unit (`1 km + 500 m` = `1.5 km`; `3 ft + 2 in` = `3.17 ft`); incompatible → no result. Number * unitless keeps unit. `5 km in mi` = `3.11 mi`. Time: `90 min in h` = `1.5 h`.
- Unit names must not shadow variables: if a variable named e.g. `m` exists, prefer the variable. A unit is only recognised directly after a number or closing paren, or after `in/to/as`.

## Formatting (Format)
- Up to 2 decimal places when |value| >= 1 and has a unit or currency; otherwise up to 6 significant decimals, trailing zeros trimmed. Thousands separators with `,` for |value| >= 10000 (e.g. `12,345.5`; `1234` stays `1234`... actually use separators for >= 1000 to match `$1,250`). Very large/small → `1.23e+15` style. Currency: `$1,250.00` style with exactly 2 decimals unless integer then `$400`. Units appended with a space: `3.11 mi`, `37.78 °C`. Percent: `25%`. NaN/∞ → no result.

## Tokens (for colouring)
Number literals (incl. currency symbol and k/M suffix) → Number; identifiers that are variables (assigned or referenced) → Identifier; unit words → Unit; `in/to/as/of/off/prev/ans/sum/total/avg/average/plus/minus/times/mod` → Keyword; function names → Function; `+-*/^%=()×÷` → Operator; comment/label text → Comment. Offsets are within the line string.

## SelfTest must cover at least
`2+3*4`=14, `2^3^2`=512, `(1+2)*3`=9, `-3^2` = -9, `10 / 4`=2.5, `1,000 + 1`=1001, `5k * 2`=10000 (display `10,000`), `0x10`=16, `50 + 10%`=55, `20% of 150`=30, `15% off 80`=68, `30 as % of 120`="25%", `sqrt(16)`=4, `round(3.14159, 2)`=3.14, `max(1,5,3)`=5, `sin(30°)`≈0.5, `2pi`≈6.283185, assignment+reuse across lines, reassignment, `prev`, `sum` block (stops at blank line), `avg`, `Coffee: 4.50` = 4.5, `# heading` no result, prose no result, `$1200 / 3` display `$400`, `$10.5 * 3` display `$31.50`, `5 km in mi` display `3.11 mi`, `1 km + 500 m` display `1.5 km`, `100 F in C` display `37.78 °C`, `2 GB in MB` = 2000, `1 GiB in MiB`=1024, `90 min in h` display `1.5 h`, `3 x 4`=12, `1/0` → no result, incompatible units no result, EvaluateInline("12*4 =") = 48, EvaluateInline("hello =") null, EvaluateInline("5 =") null (no operator), EvaluateInline("5 km in m =") = 5000 m, plus token-kind checks for a line like `rent = 1200 * 2 // monthly`.

When done, reply with: the file path, line count, the SelfTest output (pass count), and any language features you had to simplify. Keep the file self-contained and readable (comments only where non-obvious).
